# NOBORU TESTING RULE

Version: 1.0

Priority: HIGH

Status: AUTHORITATIVE

This rule governs all automated testing within Noboru.

Every feature, service, repository, workflow, and critical UI behavior must follow this document and docs/testing-strategy.md.

Tests exist to protect learners' progress and developers' velocity.

⸻

PURPOSE

The purpose of this rule is to ensure:

* Domain logic remains correct as Noboru scales
* Regressions are caught before users encounter them
* Services and repositories are verifiable in isolation
* Features ship with meaningful coverage, not token tests
* Educational, auth, and gamification boundaries remain enforced by tests
* Refactoring remains safe over years of development

Test systems.

Not only snapshots.

⸻

REQUIREMENTS

Coverage Expectations

* Every feature module includes a tests/ directory
* Services require unit tests for business rules
* Repositories require integration tests where query behavior is non-trivial
* API routes require request validation and auth behavior tests where applicable
* Critical user workflows require e2e coverage as defined in docs/testing-strategy.md

Test Types

Unit Tests

* Pure logic, service methods, validators, mappers
* Fast, isolated, deterministic

Integration Tests

* Service plus repository behavior against controlled fixtures or test database
* Auth and RLS-sensitive paths where feasible

End-to-End Tests

* Core learning, review, auth, and progression flows
* Mobile-first viewport scenarios where UI automation supports them

Feature Structure

features/feature-name/tests/

Required for all feature modules over time.

Architecture Boundaries

* Tests must verify UI does not own business logic indirectly through untested hooks
* Tests must verify gamification does not own educational progress
* Tests must verify repositories remain the only database access layer from services

Educational Integrity

* Review scheduling, progress updates, and content selection logic require tests
* Content-driven behavior must use fixtures sourced from representative domain data, not hardcoded production content in UI tests

Reference

* docs/testing-strategy.md defines tooling, CI expectations, coverage priorities, and e2e scope

States

* Tests must cover loading, success, error, and recovery paths for critical workflows where applicable

⸻

CODING STANDARDS

Organization

* Co-locate feature tests under features/*/tests/
* Shared test utilities live in tests/ or lib/test/ as established
* Name files *.test.ts or *.test.tsx consistently

Style

* Arrange, act, assert structure
* One behavioral assertion focus per test where possible
* Use descriptive test names that explain rule or workflow

Fixtures

* Prefer factory helpers over copy-pasted mock objects
* Keep educational fixtures minimal and domain-realistic

Mocking

* Mock repositories at service unit boundaries
* Do not mock the behavior under test
* Avoid over-mocking rendering libraries in unit tests

Determinism

* No flaky time-dependent tests without clock control
* No network dependence in unit tests

CI

* Tests must run in continuous integration
* Failing tests block merge for protected domains defined in docs/testing-strategy.md

⸻

SUCCESS CRITERIA

Testing work is successful when:

* Every feature has a tests/ directory or an documented exception in docs/testing-strategy.md
* Core services have unit tests for business rules
* Auth and permission failures are tested
* Educational progress workflows have regression tests
* Gamification unlock logic is tested against educational inputs
* Tests run reliably in CI
* Failures clearly identify broken domain rules

⸻

FAILURE CRITERIA

Testing quality is degraded when any of the following occur:

* Features ship with no tests folder and no approved exception
* Tests that only assert component renders without behavior
* Missing tests for SRS, progress, or auth-sensitive mutations
* Flaky e2e suites ignored instead of fixed
* Duplicated test utilities across features
* Tests that hit production databases or secrets
* Untested permission checks on user-owned mutations
* Snapshot-only coverage for critical workflows

When failure criteria appear, add tests before expanding feature scope.

⸻

EXAMPLES

Correct service unit test

review.service.test.ts verifies next interval calculation for known inputs using mocked repository.

Correct integration test

review.repository.integration.test.ts verifies user-scoped due query returns only owned rows.

Correct boundary test

achievements.service.test.ts verifies unlock reads learning summary and does not mutate educational tables.

Incorrect test

expect(screen.getByText("Noboru")).toBeInTheDocument() as sole coverage for review submission flow.

Incorrect fixture

Hardcoding entire JLPT vocabulary lists inside component tests instead of minimal fixture objects.

⸻

ANTI-PATTERNS

Testing implementation details instead of behavior

Giant e2e tests covering every edge case without unit coverage

No tests for authorization failures

Copy-pasted mocks in every feature

Tests coupled to brittle CSS selectors without role-based queries

Skipping tests for "UI-only" changes that moved business logic into hooks

Using production credentials in test suites

False confidence from 100% snapshot coverage

Testing gamification unlocks against manually edited fake progress in UI state only

